    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "837beecf-4239-4b3d-b683-1d611af3da2e",
      "key": "83cbddb8-c9d3-4e40-be12-67e79b230a31",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Itinerary"
        },
        {
          "alias": "richTextContent",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Ch3\u003EDay 1\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe depart by executive coach from our designated pick-up points and en route to our hotel we visit spend some time at leisure for lunch in Harrogate. We then continue to the 3* Gibside Hotel at Whickham for a four night stay on a half board basis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 2\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EToday we explore Hadrian\u2019s Wall Country with a Blue Badge guide where we have the opportunity to walk and see the remains of the wall which is a World Heritage Site and dates back to 122 AD. Our tour incorporates a visit to Chesters Roman Fort, one of the best preserved examples of a Roman cavalry fort in Britain. Built for 500 cavalrymen, remains of the fort include five gateways, barrack blocks, commandant\u2019s house and headquarters and the finest military bath house in Britain. We continue to Cawfields, widely acknowledged to be the most dramatic section of Hadrian\u2019s Wall, where we enjoy a short walk with our guide. The rest of the day is at leisure in Hexham.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 3\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe visit Raby Castle with our Blue Badge guide this morning. Home to Lord Barnard\u2019s family since 1626, Raby is one of the finest medieval castles in England. This afternoon we visit Hopetown Darlington. In 1825, a railway journey changed the world forever. Locomotion No.1 became the first steam locomotive to haul a passenger carrying train on a public railway \u2013 the Stockton and Darlington Railway. Darlington changed the world as a pioneer of the modern railway. Hopetown Darlington engages and inspires the innovators of the future by connecting them with our pioneering railway heritage. After an early dinner in our hotel, we travel to Kynren to experience \u2018an epic tale of England\u2019. The thrilling tale is told on an enormous scale bringing vividly to life the incredible story of England\u2019s last 2,000 years. This multi award winning live action 90 minute outdoor theatre spectacular delights and dazzles the senses. The production involves 29 dramatic scenes that take audiences on a thrilling, action packed and emotional journey through time. From the fog of war and the noise of battle to the heat of industry; from Romans to Victorians; from the lavish pomp and pageantry of royal ceremony to thrilling choreography, amazing stunts, equestrianism, special effects and pyrotechnics.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 4\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOur Blue Badge guide meets us at our hotel this morning. We firstly visit Auckland Palace, positioned high above the meandering River Wear. The palace was once home to the Prince Bishops of Durham, who were given exceptional powers by the Norman kings of England, as well as the Church, and governed vast swathes of North East England. It was built to host lavish medieval celebrations and hunting parties, to entertain royalty and impress visitors with the bishops\u2019 power and wealth. We continue to the National Trust\u2019s Seaton Delaval Hall. Recently acquired by the National Trust, Seaton Delaval Hall was built between 1719 and 1732, and the great English baroque villa was designed by Sir John Vanbrugh for Admiral George Delaval.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 5\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter checking out of our hotel and en route home we visit Wentworth Woodhouse in South Yorkshire, one of the grandest stately homes in the country. During our guided tour we discover the formal staterooms including the Marble Salon, Whistlejacket and the Painted Drawing Room. The tour encompasses the rise and fall of the house from its beginnings in the 1600s to its time as a family home and teacher training college, right through to the present day and the work of the Wentworth Woodhouse Preservation Trust. Time is then at leisure to explore the gardens, home to a wildflower meadow, ancient trees, shaded woodland copses and 18th Century follies. Taking carefully selected comfort stops en route, we return back to our original pick up points.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureDescription",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": ""
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureSummary",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003ENB: Please note we reserve the right to alter the above itinerary if required for operational reasons.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
